/* ============================================================================
arithmetic-means.epher -- insert k arithmetic means between two numbers

The arithmetic means between a and b are the k equally spaced values that
make a, m1, ..., mk, b an arithmetic sequence, so the step is
d = (b - a) / (k + 1) and mean i is a + i d (any algebra text, e.g.
Stewart, Redlin and Watson, "College Algebra", 7th ed., sec. 9.1). With
a = 2, b = 32 and k = 5 the step is 5 and the means are 7, 12, 17, 22,
27. A for loop collects them into one list. All values are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/sequences-series/arithmetic-means.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\sequences-series\arithmetic-means.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/sequences-series/arithmetic-means.epher

The knobs to edit are a, b and k below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs ---------------------------------------------------------------
const a = 2                      // start of the interval
const b = 32                     // end of the interval
const k = 5                      // how many means to insert

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
d = (b - a) / (k + 1)            // the common step (echoes 5)
for m in 1 to k do a + m * d     // the k means, echoes {7, 12, 17, 22, 27}
print("the step is the whole gap split k + 1 ways:", b - a == (k + 1) * d)
print("last mean plus the step lands on b:", a + k * d + d == b)
/* ---- expected output ----
= 2
= 32
= 5
= 5
= {7, 12, 17, 22, 27}
= the step is the whole gap split k + 1 ways: true
= last mean plus the step lands on b: true
*/
